# AUTOMATIC DIFFERENTIATION, as a model of a theory.
#
# The thesis (Elliott, *The simple essence of automatic
# differentiation*; READING): differentiation is a FUNCTOR into pairs
# of a value and a LINEAR MAP — D(f)(x) = (f x, f'(x)·—) — and it is a
# functor because the chain rule says exactly that composition goes to
# composition.  A model of a theory in Braid IS a functor out of the
# free category on the theory's generators.  So: write the arithmetic
# as a THEORY, write differentiation as a MODEL of it, and the chain
# rule is not a rule any more.  It appears NOWHERE in this file.  What
# appears instead is one slot per generator, each saying what the
# derivative of that ONE operation is; composition is the language's.
#
# Three models of one theory: `Floats` evaluates, `Fwd` carries a
# tangent (forward mode), `Rev` carries the TRANSPOSE of the same
# linear map as a continuation (reverse mode).  `Fwd` is not one model
# but a FAMILY: `model Fwd(R : Smooth(a, g))` is a functor
# Mod(Smooth) → Mod(Smooth), so `use Fwd(Fwd(Floats))` differentiates
# TWICE and §6 prints second derivatives.  Forward and reverse are
# not two algorithms here; they are two REPRESENTATIONS of one linear
# map, and `transformation Transpose : Fwd ⇒ Rev` is the sentence that
# says so — §8 declares it, and records which of its squares the
# normalizer proves and which the theory's samples decide.  A fourth
# model in §9 threads the adjoint through a resource.
#
# There is no `Code`, no `functor`, no `getCode` anywhere below.  This
# is not metaprogramming: it is one theory, four models, two declared
# transformations and a resource.  Binders throughout, so every slot body
# reads as the arithmetic it is.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The theory: a commutative ring with exp and sin, over one carrier
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `sample` is the evidence a parametric law runs on (a law cannot
# invent a value of `a`).  `observe` is an EXIT: a slot that takes the
# carrier and hands back base, so a law can compare results.  Every law
# below is stated at `observe`, because two carriers cannot be compared
# — `Rev`'s carrier holds a closure.
#
# `gradient` is the OTHER exit, and the reason the theory has two
# parameters.  An exit's result is what the model hands back, and for a
# gradient that is not one type: `Fwd` hands back a tangent `Float`,
# `Rev` a whole `Grad`.  A slot has ONE type for every model, so the
# result type is a THEORY PARAMETER the model instantiates — `gradient
# : a ⇒ g`, with `Smooth(Float, Float)`, `Smooth(Dual(a), a)` and
# `Smooth(Rev, Grad)`.  That is the general rule: an exit whose result
# varies by model is a theory parameter (§8 shows what it buys —
# `Transpose`'s gradient half becomes a square like any other).
#
# `cos` IS A GENERATOR BECAUSE `Fwd` IS A MODEL OF THIS THEORY
# (2026-09-15).  A theory closed under differentiation must be able to
# WRITE each generator's derivative in its own vocabulary: d(exp x) is
# `exp`, d(sin x) is `cos`, d(cos x) is `sin` and `neg`.  While `Fwd`
# was a hand-written model over `Float` it could reach `fcos`, a base
# word, and the theory did not have to say `cos`.  A model of `Smooth`
# parameterized by a model of `Smooth` has no base words to reach: the
# only vocabulary it has is the theory's.  So the theory grew the
# generator its own differentiation needed, which is the honest reading
# of "closed under D".
#
# `Smooth` is NOT declared `over Doctrine`.  The doctrine's carrier is
# a hom-object `k(a, b)` — a category of arrows — and this is an
# algebraic theory over ONE carrier: a ring.  Elliott's functor is
# between categories; a model of an algebraic theory is the same
# statement one level down, on the free category its generators
# present.

theory Smooth(a, g) =
    add     : a a ⇒ a
    mul     : a a ⇒ a
    neg     : a ⇒ a
    lit     : Float ⇒ a
    exp     : a ⇒ a
    sin     : a ⇒ a
    cos     : a ⇒ a
    sample  : • ⇒ a
    observe : a ⇒ Float
    gradient : a ⇒ g
    # The ring laws, at the theory's own evidence.  They are exact at
    # these points — 1.5, 2.0 and 3.0 are dyadic rationals with small
    # mantissas, so every sum and product below is a double with no
    # rounding.  At other points they would NOT be exact: `Float`
    # arithmetic is a ring only up to rounding, and stating the laws at
    # samples is the only honest way to state them at all.
    law addComm  = (sample (2.0 ; lit) ; add ; observe) ((2.0 ; lit) sample ; add ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law addAssoc = ((sample (2.0 ; lit) ; add) (3.0 ; lit) ; add ; observe) (sample ((2.0 ; lit) (3.0 ; lit) ; add) ; add ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law mulComm  = (sample (2.0 ; lit) ; mul ; observe) ((2.0 ; lit) sample ; mul ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law mulAssoc = ((sample (2.0 ; lit) ; mul) (3.0 ; lit) ; mul ; observe) (sample ((2.0 ; lit) (3.0 ; lit) ; mul) ; mul ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law distrib  = ((sample (2.0 ; lit) ; add) (3.0 ; lit) ; mul ; observe) ((sample (3.0 ; lit) ; mul) ((2.0 ; lit) (3.0 ; lit) ; mul) ; add ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law addUnit  = (sample (0.0 ; lit) ; add ; observe) (sample ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law mulUnit  = (sample (1.0 ; lit) ; mul ; observe) (sample ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law negAdd   = (sample (sample ; neg) ; add ; observe) (0.0 ; lit ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Evaluation — the model everything else is compared against
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The carrier is `Float` itself, so `lit` and `observe` are both the
# identity.  This is the model that says what the programs MEAN.

# ...and its gradient is the ZERO MAP.  `Floats` carries no dependence
# on anything: every carrier value it holds is a constant, and the
# derivative of a constant is 0.  That is not a placeholder — it is what
# makes `Value` below a homomorphism at `g` as well as at `a`:
# forgetting a tangent zeroes it.
def zeroTangent = (d -> 0.0)

model Floats : Smooth(Float, Float) =
    add     = fadd
    mul     = fmul
    neg     = fneg
    lit     = id
    exp     = fexp
    sin     = fsin
    cos     = fcos
    sample  = 1.5
    observe = id
    gradient = zeroTangent

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Forward mode — the value, paired with a tangent, over ANY model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Dual(a)` is (value, tangent) OVER A CARRIER: the value of the
# expression and its derivative along ONE seeded direction, both held at
# whatever type the model underneath uses.  Each slot says what the
# derivative of that one operation is, LOCALLY — `d(a·b) = a·db + da·b`
# is written once, in `mul`, and nothing anywhere composes derivatives
# by hand.
#
# `model Fwd(R : Smooth(a, g))` is A MODEL PARAMETERIZED BY A MODEL, and
# it is not one model: it is a FAMILY, a functor Mod(Smooth) →
# Mod(Smooth) (Kiselyov's interpreter transformers; ML's higher-order
# functors; the ring construction R ↦ R[ε]/ε²).  `use Fwd(Floats)`
# applies it and mints the member — an ordinary model named
# `Fwd(Floats)`, with ordinary slots and the theory's laws run at its
# own evidence.
#
# INSIDE A SLOT BODY EVERY THEORY NAME IS R'S.  The bodies are TEMPLATES
# over the parameter's theory, so `add` in `add`'s own body is R's `add`
# and nothing else — the slot being defined is not in scope in its own
# body, which is what makes `add = … add …` unambiguous with no
# self-reference rule to learn.
#
# THE TANGENT OF A LITERAL IS `0.0 ; lit`, not a new `zero` slot.  The
# theory already names its zero, and `law addUnit` above already pins it
# — `(sample (0.0 ; lit) ; add ; observe)` is `sample`.  A `zero : • ⇒ a`
# slot would be a second spelling of a thing the theory has, and at
# `Fwd(Fwd(Floats))` the two spellings must agree anyway: `0.0 ; lit` at
# `Fwd(Floats)` is `Dual(0.0, 0.0)`, which is exactly the zero of the
# first-order duals.
#
# THE GRADIENT TYPE FALLS OUT.  `Fwd(R)`'s `g` is R's CARRIER, not R's
# `g`: the head reads `Smooth(Dual(a), a)`, and the `gradient` slot is
# the projection `Dual(v, t) -> t`.  At `Fwd(Floats)` that is `Float`,
# the same exit the hand-written model had; at `Fwd(Fwd(Floats))` it is
# `Dual(Float)` — a first derivative that carries its own tangent, which
# is the second derivative, and §6 reads it with `tangent ; tangent`.
# Nothing has to be asked of R for this: a tangent lives in the carrier.
#
# Seed the input with tangent `1.0 ; lit` and the tangent that comes out
# IS the derivative: `(x (1.0 ; lit) ; Dual)` is `x` with `dx/dx = 1`.

data Dual(a) = a a

model Fwd(R : Smooth(a, g)) : Smooth(Dual(a), a) =
    add = Dual(x, dx) Dual(y, dy) -> (x y ; add) (dx dy ; add) ; Dual
    mul = Dual(x, dx) Dual(y, dy) -> (x y ; mul) ((x dy ; mul) (dx y ; mul) ; add) ; Dual
    neg = Dual(x, dx) -> (x ; neg) (dx ; neg) ; Dual
    # a literal is constant, so its tangent is the carrier's zero
    lit = (c -> (c ; lit) (0.0 ; lit) ; Dual)
    # d(exp x) = exp x · dx, and the value is shared with the tangent
    exp = Dual(x, dx) -> (x ; exp) ; (e -> e (e dx ; mul) ; Dual)
    # d(sin x) = cos x · dx, d(cos x) = −sin x · dx — both in R's words
    sin = Dual(x, dx) -> (x ; sin) ((x ; cos) dx ; mul) ; Dual
    cos = Dual(x, dx) -> (x ; cos) ((x ; sin ; neg) dx ; mul) ; Dual
    sample  = sample (1.0 ; lit) ; Dual
    observe = Dual(v, t) -> v ; observe
    gradient = Dual(v, t) -> t

def value   = Dual(v, t) -> v
def tangent = Dual(v, t) -> t
#   value, tangent : Dual(a) ⇒ a — ordinary words over any carrier

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Reverse mode — the value, paired with the TRANSPOSE of that map
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Grad` is a FIXED TWO-SLOT gradient record.  A gradient wants to be a
# map from input names to partials; Braid has no such data structure
# and building one is not this file's job, so the record is two slots,
# `gx` and `gy` seed them, and `gadd` adds two of them.  That is enough
# for a two-input function and it is exactly as far as it goes.
#
# A `Rev` is the value and a CONTINUATION `Float ⇒ Grad`: given the
# adjoint of this node (∂output/∂here), it returns the gradient of the
# INPUTS.  That is the transpose of the linear map `Fwd` carries —
# forward pushes a tangent along the arrow, reverse pulls an adjoint
# back — and the slots build it during the forward pass.
#
# FAN-OUT NEEDS NO SPECIAL CASE.  Every continuation is LINEAR, so a
# value used twice contributes twice, and calling its continuation
# twice and adding is exactly right.  `k2` below is where that happens
# and it is the whole of it: no tape, no node ids, no visit counting.

data Grad = Float Float
data Rev  = Float Fn⟨Float ⇒ Grad⟩

def gzero = 0.0 0.0 ; Grad
def gx    = (d -> d 0.0 ; Grad)
def gy    = (d -> 0.0 d ; Grad)
def gadd  = Grad(u, v) Grad(s, t) -> (u s ; fadd) (v t ; fadd) ; Grad

# two continuations, each given its own share of the adjoint, summed.
# Linearity is what makes this a sum rather than a merge.
def k2 = (ka da kb db -> ka da ; ev ; (ga -> kb db ; ev ; (gb -> ga gb ; gadd)))

# the linear map "scale by dx", as a continuation into the x slot
def scaleK = (dx -> [(d -> (d dx ; fmul) ; gx)])

def radd = Rev(a, ka) Rev(b, kb) -> (a b ; fadd) [(d -> ka d kb d ; k2)] ; Rev
def rmul = Rev(a, ka) Rev(b, kb) -> (a b ; fmul) \
    [(d -> ka (d b ; fmul) kb (d a ; fmul) ; k2)] ; Rev
def rneg = Rev(a, ka) -> (a ; fneg) [(d -> ka (d ; fneg) ; ev)] ; Rev
# a literal depends on no input: its continuation is the ZERO linear
# map, which is `scaleK` at 0.0 — the same map `[(d -> gzero)]` is, and
# the same CODE the `lit` square's other side normalizes to (§8)
def rlit = (c -> c (0.0 ; scaleK) ; Rev)
def rexp = Rev(a, ka) -> (a ; fexp) ; (e -> e [(d -> ka (d e ; fmul) ; ev)] ; Rev)
def rsin = Rev(a, ka) -> (a ; fsin) [(d -> ka (d (a ; fcos) ; fmul) ; ev)] ; Rev
def rcos = Rev(a, ka) -> (a ; fcos) \
    [(d -> ka (d (a ; fsin ; fneg) ; fmul) ; ev)] ; Rev
def rvalue = Rev(v, k) -> v

# THE EXIT PROBLEM, and how it is resolved.
#
# A theory's exit is a SLOT, so it has ONE type for every model.  `Rev`
# has two things to hand back — the value and the gradient — and until
# 2026-09-14 the theory could not ask for the second: `observe : a ⇒
# Float` fits `Fwd`'s tangent and not `Rev`'s `Grad`, so the gradient
# was read by an ordinary base word OUTSIDE the theory, and the
# gradient half of `Transpose` was not machine-checked at all.
#
# The answer is not a per-model slot type; it is that the exit's RESULT
# is a theory parameter.  `gradient : a ⇒ g` is one slot with one type,
# and `g` is filled per model — `Float` for `Fwd`, `Grad` for `Rev`.
# `backward` is now that slot's binding for `Rev`: it runs the
# accumulated continuation at adjoint 1.0 — "the derivative of the
# output with respect to itself is 1" — and that is the whole of the
# reverse sweep.
def backward = Rev(v, k) -> k 1.0 ; ev

# the two input seeds: x is "the thing whose adjoint lands in slot 1"
def seedX = (x -> x [gx] ; Rev)
def seedY = (y -> y [gy] ; Rev)

model Rev : Smooth(Rev, Grad) =
    add     = radd
    mul     = rmul
    neg     = rneg
    lit     = rlit
    exp     = rexp
    sin     = rsin
    cos     = rcos
    # the transpose of `Fwd`'s own sample: value 1.5, tangent 1.0
    sample  = 1.5 (1.0 ; scaleK) ; Rev
    observe = rvalue
    gradient = backward

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Programs, written ONCE, over the theory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `over Smooth` makes each of these a TEMPLATE: a morphism of the
# theory, waiting for a model.  Nothing here knows what a derivative
# is.

#   p(x) = x³ − 2x + 1
def poly = over Smooth ;
    (x -> ((x x ; mul) x ; mul) ((2.0 ; lit) x ; mul ; neg) ; add
        ; (1.0 ; lit) ... ; add)
#   w(x) = sin(2x²)
def wave = over Smooth ; (x -> (2.0 ; lit) (x x ; mul) ; mul ; sin)
#   f(x, y) = x·y + sin x
def fxy  = over Smooth ; (x y -> (x y ; mul) (x ; sin) ; add)

def polyF = use Floats ; poly
def polyD = use Fwd(Floats) ; poly
def polyR = use Rev    ; poly
def waveF = use Floats ; wave
def waveD = use Fwd(Floats) ; wave
def waveR = use Rev    ; wave
def fxyF  = use Floats ; fxy
def fxyD  = use Fwd(Floats) ; fxy
def fxyR  = use Rev    ; fxy
#   polyF : Float =Floats> Float,
#   polyD : Dual(Float) =Fwd(Floats)> Dual(Float),
#   polyR : Rev =Rev> Rev — one body, three principal types, and the
#   label on each arrow is the RECEIPT of the model that read it.

"-- p(x) = x^3 - 2x + 1, at x = 2 --" ; print
2.0 ; polyF ; print                                       # 5.0     = 8 - 4 + 1
(2.0 1.0 ; Dual) ; polyD ; unDual ; print ... ; print      # 5.0, then
                                                          # 10.0    = 3*4 - 2
(2.0 ; seedX) ; polyR ; rvalue ; print                     # 5.0
(2.0 ; seedX) ; polyR ; backward ; unGrad ; print ... ; print   # 10.0, then 0.0
                                                          # (nothing depends on y)

"-- w(x) = sin(2x^2), at x = 2 --" ; print
2.0 ; waveF ; print                       # 0.9893582466233818  = sin 8
(2.0 1.0 ; Dual) ; waveD ; tangent ; print
                                          # -1.1640002704689083 = 8*cos 8,
                                          #   cos 8 = -0.14550003380861354
(2.0 ; seedX) ; waveR ; backward ; unGrad ; print ... ; print
                                          # -1.1640002704689083, then 0.0

"-- f(x, y) = x*y + sin x, at (2, 3) --" ; print
2.0 3.0 ; fxyF ; print                    # 6.909297426825682  = 6 + sin 2,
                                          #   sin 2 = 0.9092974268256817
(2.0 1.0 ; Dual) (3.0 0.0 ; Dual) ; fxyD ; tangent ; print
                                          # 2.5838531634528574 = y + cos x
                                          #   = 3 - 0.4161468365471424
(2.0 0.0 ; Dual) (3.0 1.0 ; Dual) ; fxyD ; tangent ; print   # 2.0  = x
# ONE reverse sweep gives BOTH partials; forward mode needed two runs.
(2.0 ; seedX) (3.0 ; seedY) ; fxyR ; backward ; unGrad ; print ... ; print
                                          # 2.5838531634528574, then 2.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. SECOND DERIVATIVES — the same family, applied to itself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Fwd` is a functor Mod(Smooth) → Mod(Smooth), so it can be applied to
# a model it made.  `use Fwd(Fwd(Floats))` mints the model whose carrier
# is `Dual(Dual(Float))` and reads the SAME three templates — `poly`,
# `wave` and `fxy` are not touched, and nothing below knows there is a
# second derivative in the world.
#
# HOW TO READ A NESTED DUAL.  The outer level's value is the whole
# first-order run and the outer level's tangent is its derivative, so
# for a one-input function the four projections are
#
#     value   ; value    f(x)          tangent ; value    f′(x)
#     value   ; tangent  f′(x)         tangent ; tangent  f″(x)
#
# and the two readings of f′ agreeing is the symmetry of the second
# derivative, printed rather than asserted.
#
# THE SEED IS THE SAME SENTENCE TWICE: "this wire is the one I am
# differentiating along".  At the inner level that is tangent `1.0`; at
# the outer level it is tangent `Dual(1.0, 0.0)` — the constant one,
# whose own derivative is zero.
def seed2 = (x -> (x 1.0 ; Dual) (1.0 0.0 ; Dual) ; Dual)
#   seed2 : Float ⇒ Dual(Dual(Float))

def polyDD = use Fwd(Fwd(Floats)) ; poly
def waveDD = use Fwd(Fwd(Floats)) ; wave
def fxyDD  = use Fwd(Fwd(Floats)) ; fxy
#   polyDD : Dual(Dual(Float)) =Fwd(Fwd(Floats))> Dual(Dual(Float)) —
#   ONE receipt, `Fwd(Fwd(Floats))`, because ONE model read the
#   template.  `Floats` never saw it.

"-- p(x) = x^3 - 2x + 1 twice, at x = 2 --" ; print
2.0 ; seed2 ; polyDD ; value ; value ; print       # 5.0   = 8 - 4 + 1
2.0 ; seed2 ; polyDD ; value ; tangent ; print     # 10.0  = 3*4 - 2
2.0 ; seed2 ; polyDD ; tangent ; value ; print     # 10.0  — the same p'(2),
                                                   #   read down the other
                                                   #   diagonal
2.0 ; seed2 ; polyDD ; tangent ; tangent ; print   # 12.0  = p''(2) = 6*2
#   p'(x) = 3x^2 - 2 and p''(x) = 6x, both by hand; the file computes
#   neither and differentiates the same `poly` twice.

"-- w(x) = sin(2x^2) twice, at x = 2 --" ; print
2.0 ; seed2 ; waveDD ; tangent ; value ; print
                                          # -1.1640002704689083 = 8*cos 8,
                                          #   the number §5 printed
2.0 ; seed2 ; waveDD ; tangent ; tangent ; print
                                          # w''(x) = 4*cos(2x^2)
                                          #        - 16x^2*sin(2x^2)
                                          #   = 4*(-0.14550003380861354)
                                          #   - 64*0.9893582466233818
                                          #   = -63.900927919130886

# A MIXED PARTIAL, by seeding the two levels in DIFFERENT directions:
# the inner tangent moves x, the outer one moves y, so `tangent ;
# tangent` is d2f/dxdy.  f(x, y) = x*y + sin x, so that is 1 exactly,
# and it is the one second derivative of this file a reader can check
# without a calculator.
def seedXX = (x -> (x 1.0 ; Dual) (0.0 0.0 ; Dual) ; Dual)
def seedYY = (y -> (y 0.0 ; Dual) (1.0 0.0 ; Dual) ; Dual)

"-- d2/dxdy of f(x, y) = x*y + sin x, at (2, 3) --" ; print
(2.0 ; seedXX) (3.0 ; seedYY) ; fxyDD ; tangent ; tangent ; print   # 1.0
(2.0 ; seed2) (3.0 ; seedYY) ; fxyDD ; value ; value ; print
                                          # 6.909297426825682 — the value
                                          #   §5 printed, unchanged by how
                                          #   the tangents were seeded

# THE LAWS RUN AGAIN, HERE.  A parameterized model's laws cannot be
# checked once for the family: that would need equality modulo the
# parameter theory's laws, and the checker has no such thing.  They are
# checked AT EACH INSTANTIATION, on the instantiated model's own
# evidence — `Fwd(Floats)` runs the eight ring laws at `Dual(1.5, 1.0)`
# and `Fwd(Fwd(Floats))` runs them again at `Dual(Dual(1.5, 1.0),
# Dual(1.0, 0.0))`.  A false family is therefore refused at its FIRST
# instantiation, and the refusal names the member — `law 'mulAssoc'
# fails for model Fwd(Floats)` names the family and the argument at
# once, because the applied form IS the name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Newton's method, under `Fwd`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# x ← x − p(x)/p′(x), with BOTH numbers coming out of one evaluation of
# `polyD` — which is the reason to have a derivative at all.  `use
# Recursive` puts `newton` in scope in its own body; the tolerance test
# is `fabs` and `flt?`, because `eq?` on Floats is exact and exactness
# is not what a root finder wants.

def step = Dual(v, d) -> v d ; fdiv

def close? = (r -> (r ; value ; fabs) 0.0000001 ; flt?)

def newton =
    use Recursive
    x ->
    (x 1.0 ; Dual) ; polyD
    (r -> r ; close?
       ; ((s t -> x) | (s t -> x (r ; step) ; fsub ; newton))
       ; merge)
#   newton : Float =Fwd Recursive> Float

"-- a root of p, from x0 = 1.5 --" ; print
1.5 ; newton ; print           # 1.0000000000000777 — p(1) = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0,
                               #   so the root is 1.0 and this is it to
                               #   within the tolerance asked for
1.5 ; newton ; polyF ; print   # 0.00000000000007771561172376096 — under 1e-7.
                               #   The display never uses exponent notation,
                               #   so a small number is long; it is also
                               #   exactly what it says it is.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. The transformations — two declarations, and what their squares
#    establish
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORGETTING THE TANGENT IS A HOMOMORPHISM.  That single declaration is
# the statement "AD computes the right value": for every generator, the
# square
#
#     Fwd(Floats)@s ; Value  =  Value…Value ; Floats@s
#
# commutes.  All TEN of them — add, mul, neg, lit, exp, sin, cos,
# sample, observe and gradient — are PROVED by the normalizer, for every input, not
# sampled.  A `Dual` is two Float wires with a roll around it, so
# `unDual` partitions into one track and the whole square normalizes.

transformation Value : Fwd(Floats) ⇒ Floats = value, zeroTangent
#   A TRANSFORMATION NAMES A MODEL, and `Fwd(Floats)` is a model — which
#   is the reason the instantiation spelling is an APPLICATION and not a
#   scope stack: the applied form is the model's NAME, so it is writable
#   here, printable on an arrow, and the same in both places.
#
#   ONE COMPONENT PER THEORY PARAMETER, in the theory's order: `value :
#   Dual(Float) ⇒ Float` at `a`, `zeroTangent : Float ⇒ Float` at `g`.  Both
#   are ordinary words.  The second is what the `gradient` square needs:
#   its two ends live at different parameters (`gradient : a ⇒ g`), so
#   one component cannot draw that square.

# A TANGENT BECOMES THE LINEAR MAP THAT SCALES BY IT.  That is the
# sentence "forward and reverse are two representations of the same
# linear map", and it is a declaration too, since 2026-09-14.
def transpose = Dual(x, dx) -> x (dx ; scaleK) ; Rev
#   transpose : Dual(Float) ⇒ Rev

transformation Transpose : Fwd(Floats) ⇒ Rev = transpose, gx
#   at `g`, Fwd's tangent `Float` becomes Rev's `Grad`, and the map is
#   `gx` — the tangent was seeded at x, so the gradient it represents is
#   the one with that partial in slot 1.  Nothing new is defined for it.

# What its TEN squares establish (`:transformations` in the REPL
# prints the verdicts): `lit`, `sample` and `observe` are PROVED by the
# normalizer, for every input — which is why `rlit` builds the ZERO
# LINEAR MAP `(0.0 ; scaleK)` rather than `[(d -> gzero)]`: the two are
# the same map and only the first is the same CODE, and `lit`'s input
# is a `Float` rather than the carrier, so no `sample` reaches it and a
# proof is the only verdict available.  The other seven — `add`, `mul`,
# `neg`, `exp`, `sin`, `cos` and `gradient` — are SAMPLED at the theory's
# evidence.  `:transformations` says WHY each needed the samples:
# `differ in the free category`.  `sameCode` does not refuse them — it
# DECIDES them false, because the two sides are equal only up to
# associativity and distributivity of `fadd` and `fmul`, and those are
# uninterpreted words to the normalizer.  A `false` about the free
# category is not a `false` about a ring, which is exactly what the
# theory's evidence is for.
#
# The `gradient` square is the one that was missing until 2026-09-14,
# and it is the interesting half: `tangent ; gx` against `transpose ;
# backward` says that Fwd's tangent and Rev's accumulated continuation
# ARE the same linear map, read two ways.  It is compared at `Grad`
# rather than through an exit — a `Grad` is two Floats and `eq?`
# reaches them — where the five carrier squares go through `observe`,
# since a `Rev` holds a closure and `eq?` on a closure is syntactic.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. The same functor with the adjoint THREADED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Rev` above SUMS: every node returns a gradient and `gadd` merges
# them on the way back.  The alternative is to thread one accumulator
# through the whole reverse sweep and let the LEAVES add their adjoint
# into it.  That accumulator is a resource, and the continuations then
# return nothing at all.
#
# Note what has to be written out: `Fn⟨Gradient Float ⇒ Gradient⟩`.
# The `=Gradient>` fold is display and does not run backwards, so a
# `data` field that is a resource-threading function names the wire.

resource Gradient = Grad
data RevG = Float Fn⟨Gradient Float ⇒ Gradient⟩

def emit = unGradient _ ; gadd ; Gradient          # Grad =Gradient> •
def kx = [(gr d -> gr (d ; gx) ; emit)]
def ky = [(gr d -> gr (d ; gy) ; emit)]

def tadd = RevG(a, ka) RevG(b, kb) -> (a b ; fadd) \
    [(gr d -> ka gr d ; ev ; (g2 -> kb g2 d ; ev))] ; RevG
def tmul = RevG(a, ka) RevG(b, kb) -> (a b ; fmul) \
    [(gr d -> ka gr (d b ; fmul) ; ev ; (g2 -> kb g2 (d a ; fmul) ; ev))] ; RevG
def tneg = RevG(a, ka) -> (a ; fneg) [(gr d -> ka gr (d ; fneg) ; ev)] ; RevG
def tlit = (c -> c [(gr d -> gr)] ; RevG)
def texp = RevG(a, ka) -> (a ; fexp) ; (e -> e [(gr d -> ka gr (d e ; fmul) ; ev)] ; RevG)
def tsin = RevG(a, ka) -> (a ; fsin) [(gr d -> ka gr (d (a ; fcos) ; fmul) ; ev)] ; RevG
def tcos = RevG(a, ka) -> (a ; fcos) \
    [(gr d -> ka gr (d (a ; fsin ; fneg) ; fmul) ; ev)] ; RevG
def tvalue = RevG(v, k) -> v
def tseedX = (x -> x kx ; RevG)

model RevThread : Smooth(RevG, Grad) =
    add     = tadd
    mul     = tmul
    neg     = tneg
    lit     = tlit
    exp     = texp
    sin     = tsin
    cos     = tcos
    sample  = 1.5 kx ; RevG
    observe = tvalue
    gradient = tgradient

# the theory's `gradient` exit, for this model: thread a fresh
# accumulator through the sweep and unroll it.  `g` is `Grad` here too,
# and it is the same `Grad` the summing model hands back.
def tgradient = RevG(v, k) -> k (gzero ; Gradient) 1.0 ; ev ; unGradient

def polyT = use RevThread ; poly
# run the reverse sweep at adjoint 1.0, threading the accumulator
def discharge = (gr RevG(v, k) -> k gr 1.0 ; ev)

def gradPoly =
    use Gradient
    tseedX
    polyT
    discharge
#   gradPoly : Float ρ0 =RevThread Gradient> ρ0 — TWO receipts on one
#   arrow: which model read the template, and which resource the scope
#   threaded.  The tail is open because a routed scope pads with `...`.

"-- the same gradient, threaded instead of summed --" ; print
(gzero ; Gradient) 2.0 ; gradPoly ; unGradient ; unGrad ; print ... ; print
                                          # 10.0, then 0.0 — the same two
                                          # numbers §5 printed for polyR
# installed at the site with `gzero`, discharged with `unGradient`.
# There is no handler keyword: a resource is installed by rolling one
# and discharged by unrolling it, which is all `metered.braid` does too.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. What is NOT built, plainly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECOND DERIVATIVES.  Nesting `Fwd` inside itself needs `Fwd` to be a
# model of `Smooth(Dual(a))` for ANY model of `Smooth(a)` — a model
# PARAMETERIZED BY A MODEL.  Braid has templates parameterized by a
# model (`over Smooth`), but a `model` head names a declared type, not
# a model variable, so `model Fwd(M) : Smooth(Dual(M))` cannot be
# written.  Differentiating twice here means writing the second model
# out by hand.
#
# THE PER-NODE ACCUMULATOR.  Wang & Rompf's shift/reset backpropagator
# (READING) gives each node ONE accumulator cell visited once, rather
# than a continuation called once per use.  That wants a reified graph
# — a map from node ids to partial sums — which is a data structure,
# and no data structure is built here.  §9's resource threads ONE
# accumulator for the whole sweep, which is the same trick at the
# leaves and not at the nodes.
#
# THE GRADIENT RECORD IS TWO SLOTS.  `Grad` is fixed at x and y.  A
# real gradient is indexed by the inputs; `examples/index.braid` has
# the machinery (`Fin(n)`, `at`, bundles) and joining it to this is a
# different piece of work.
#
# (THE GRADIENT HALF OF THE TRANSPOSE used to be listed here as not
# machine-checked.  It is checked since 2026-09-14: `gradient : a ⇒ g`
# is a slot whose result type is a theory parameter, and its square is
# decided like any other — §8.)
#
# `examples/transpose.braid` is the linear-algebra view of the same
# operation: reversing a diagram and dualizing each generator
# (copy ↔ add) is transposition, done there on reflected `Code`.  This
# file does it on VALUES instead, and the two never meet.
